      thumbnail_url: IMG('photo-1559757148-5c350d0d3c56'),
      objectives: ["Describe the structure of an atom", "Work out electron arrangements and periodic table positions", "Explain ionic and covalent bonding", "Balance simple chemical equations"],
      requirements: ["Basic arithmetic"],
      lessons: lessons("chemistry-atoms-and-bonding", [
        "Module 1: Atoms and the periodic table",
        ["Inside the atom", "text", 600, "## Building blocks of matter\nEvery atom has a tiny **nucleus** in the centre with **electrons** moving around it in shells.\n\n| Particle | Charge | Where | Relative mass |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Proton | +1 | nucleus | 1 |\n| Neutron | 0 | nucleus | 1 |\n| Electron | −1 | shells | almost 0 |\n\n- **Atomic number** = number of protons\n- **Mass number** = protons + neutrons\n- A neutral atom has equal protons and electrons.\n\n> Example: Sodium (Na) has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. It has **11 protons, 12 neutrons and 11 electrons**.\n\n> Try it: How many neutrons does carbon-14 have? (carbon's atomic number is 6) || 8", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "The atomic number of an element is the number of...", "options": ["Neutrons", "Protons", "Electron shells", "Protons plus neutrons"], "answer": 1, "why": "Atomic number = protons."}, {"prompt": "Carbon-14 (atomic number 6) has how many neutrons?", "options": ["6", "8", "14", "20"], "answer": 1, "why": "14 − 6 = 8."}, {"prompt": "A neutral atom always has equal numbers of...", "options": ["Protons and neutrons", "Neutrons and electrons", "Protons and electrons", "Shells and protons"], "answer": 2, "why": "Positive and negative charges balance."}]}],
        ["Electron arrangement and the periodic table", "text", 660, "## Filling the shells\nFor the first 20 elements the shells hold **2, 8, 8** electrons.\n\n> Example: Chlorine (atomic number 17) is **2, 8, 7**: three shells, so **Period 3**, and seven outer electrons, so **Group 7**.\n\n## Reading the table\n- **Group** number = number of outer electrons (Groups 1 to 7)\n- **Period** number = number of shells\n\n| Group | Name | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Alkali metals | Very reactive metals |\n| 7 | Halogens | Reactive non-metals |\n| 0 | Noble gases | Unreactive: full outer shell |\n\n> Try it: Write the electron arrangement of magnesium (atomic number 12). || 2, 8, 2", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "The electron arrangement of magnesium (12) is...", "options": ["2, 8", "2, 8, 2", "2, 10", "8, 2, 2"], "answer": 1, "why": "2 + 8 + 2 = 12."}, {"prompt": "Elements in the same group have the same number of...", "options": ["Shells", "Neutrons", "Outer electrons", "Protons"], "answer": 2, "why": "Outer electrons decide the group."}, {"prompt": "Noble gases are unreactive because they have...", "options": ["One outer electron", "A full outer shell", "No electrons", "Many neutrons"], "answer": 1, "why": "A full outer shell is stable."}]}],
        "Module 2: Bonding and reactions",
        ["Ionic and covalent bonding", "text", 720, "## Why atoms bond\nAtoms bond to get a full outer shell.\n\n## Ionic bonding\nA **metal** gives electrons to a **non-metal**, making charged particles called **ions**.\n> Example: Sodium loses 1 electron to become Na⁺. Chlorine gains it to become Cl⁻. They attract to form **sodium chloride, NaCl**.\n\n## Covalent bonding\nTwo **non-metals** **share** pairs of electrons, e.g. water (H₂O) and carbon dioxide (CO₂).\n\n| Property | Ionic compounds | Simple covalent |\n|---|---|---|\n| Melting point | High | Low |\n| Conduct electricity | When molten or dissolved | Never |\n\n> Try it: A substance melts at a low temperature and never conducts electricity. Which bonding does it probably have? || Simple covalent", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "The bonding in sodium chloride (NaCl) is...", "options": ["Covalent", "Ionic", "Metallic", "Hydrogen"], "answer": 1, "why": "A metal and a non-metal form an ionic compound."}, {"prompt": "Covalent bonds are formed when atoms...", "options": ["Transfer electrons", "Share electrons", "Lose neutrons", "Gain protons"], "answer": 1, "why": "Covalent = sharing."}, {"prompt": "A solid with a low melting point that does not conduct is most likely...", "options": ["Ionic", "Simple covalent", "A metal", "A noble gas ion"], "answer": 1, "why": "Simple covalent substances have weak forces between molecules."}]}],
        ["Chemical equations and balancing", "text", 660, "## Atoms are never lost\nIn a reaction atoms are only rearranged, so each side of the equation must have the **same number of each atom**.\n\n{{flow Write the correct formulas > Count the atoms on each side > Add numbers in front (coefficients) > Recheck every atom}}\n\n> Example: hydrogen + oxygen → water\n> Unbalanced: H₂ + O₂ → H₂O\n> Balanced: **2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O**\n\n> Tip: Change only the **big numbers in front**, never the small subscripts, because that would change the substance.\n\n> Try it: Balance Mg + O₂ → MgO. || 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "Balancing equations obeys the law of...", "options": ["Conservation of mass", "Gravity", "Definite volume", "Inertia"], "answer": 0, "why": "Mass (atoms) is conserved."}, {"prompt": "The balanced form of H₂ + Cl₂ → HCl is...", "options": ["H₂ + Cl₂ → HCl", "H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl", "2H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl", "H + Cl → HCl"], "answer": 1, "why": "Two H and two Cl on each side."}, {"prompt": "To balance an equation you change the...", "options": ["Subscripts", "Coefficients", "Formulas", "Products"], "answer": 1, "why": "Only coefficients may change."}]}],
      ]),
